# Existence and uniqueness of the ordinary p-root

Theorem statement: `MTT.ordinary_root_exists_unique` (`ba0388ba-8d24-4ebd-86a9-990686c1ef3a`), by
davidloeffler, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `94bc7430-5043-478a-ac90-eb6a3c6f3939`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

If the p-th eigenvalue is a p-adic unit, the polynomial X²−a_p X+ε(p)p^(k−1) has exactly one root of
p-adic norm one. Characters and coefficients are transported through the fixed algebraic-to-p-adic
embedding. This includes p dividing the level, where the constant term vanishes.

## Explanation of the source proof

For a prime $p$, weight $k\ge2$, and an ordinary eigenform, we prove
$$\exists!\,\alpha\in\mathbf C_p:\quad |\alpha|_p=1,\qquad
\alpha^2-a_p\alpha+\epsilon(p)p^{k-1}=0.$$
Here the algebraic coefficients are mapped to $\mathbf C_p$ by the specified embedding. This is the
ordinary-root assertion in Mazur–Tate–Teitelbaum, Chapter I, §12.

The argument uses the sum and product of the two roots. More generally, in an algebraically closed
ultrametric normed field of characteristic zero, suppose
$$|a|=1,\qquad |b|<1.$$
Choose a square root of the discriminant and put
$$x=(a+\sqrt{a^2-4b})/2,\qquad y=(a-\sqrt{a^2-4b})/2.$$
Then $x+y=a$ and $xy=b$. Since $|x||y|<1$, at least one root, say $x$, has norm less than one. The
ultrametric inequality applied to $y=a-x$ gives $|y|\le1$. Applied to $a=x+y$, it gives $|y|\ge1$.
Thus $|y|=1$. Every root $z$ satisfies
$$(z-x)(z-y)=0,$$
so it equals $x$ or $y$; only $y$ has norm one.

For the Hecke polynomial, values of the embedded Dirichlet character have norm at most one. Also
$|p|_p=p^{-1}<1$ and $k-1>0$, hence
$$|\epsilon(p)p^{k-1}|_p<1.$$
Ordinarity supplies $|a_p|_p=1$, so the general argument applies. This includes $p\mid N$, when the
constant coefficient can vanish, and includes $p=2$; division by $2$ is used only in the
characteristic-zero field, without any assertion that $2$ is a unit of its valuation ring.

